Colleagues,
I executed 2-hour runs of 4KRR to understand performance changes across the=
 time on the specific very fast NVMe SSD with 1.6TB capacity.
I noticed the side effect of =93norandommap=94 parameter performing full sp=
an test on the block device.
Here is the example of the result with random map (I.e. without "norandomma=
p" option) within 120 minutes windows.
[cid:E6872B64-35D1-4447-A0CF-32E6411D9BDB]
(IOPS in blue)

As soon as I enabled =93norandommap=94 option the curve has changed into th=
e straight line as expected.

Some technical details:
I=92m running Centos 7 with 3.18 kernel, SSD of course in the precondition =
state. FIO 2.2.2 (I unfortunately got higher CPU utilization with 2.2.4 whi=
ch I=92ll report separately).

Config file:
[global]
name=3D4k random read
filename=3D/dev/nvme0n1
ioengine=3Dlibaio
direct=3D1
bs=3D4k
rw=3Drandread
iodepth=3D16
numjobs=3D8
buffered=3D0
size=3D100%
randrepeat=3D0
norandommap
refill_buffers
group_reporting
[job1]


